const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — The FIR That Could Not Be Refused: Lalita Kumari"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What criminal procedure is for · Inquisitorial vs adversarial systems · Articles 14, 20, 21, 22 · Salient features of the Code · The police-prosecution-court-prison machinery · Key definitions (Sec 2) · FIR and the start of investigation (Sec 154-157)"));
c.push(STORY("Uttar Pradesh, 2008. A minor girl goes missing. Her father, Bhola Kamat, walks into the local police station with a written complaint. The officer in charge takes the paper, but no First Information Report is registered. Days pass. The father runs to the Superintendent of Police; only then is an FIR written, and even then no real search begins. He files a writ petition in the Supreme Court in the girl's name, Lalita Kumari, asking one question that had divided High Courts for decades: when a person walks in and reports a cognizable offence, does the police officer have a choice? In 2013 a Constitution Bench of five judges answered with a single word that every law student must remember: no. Section 154 uses 'shall'. If the information discloses a cognizable offence, registration of the FIR is mandatory. The officer cannot first check whether the story is true; the truth is for investigation, not for the doorstep. The Court allowed a short preliminary enquiry only in a narrow list of cases — matrimonial disputes, commercial offences, medical negligence, corruption, and abnormal delay — and even then only to find out whether a cognizable offence is disclosed, never to test the merits, and it must finish within seven days with reasons recorded in the general diary. The father's paper became the law of first contact between citizen and police."));
c.push(H3("Why procedure exists"));
c.push(P("Substantive criminal law (the Penal Code) tells us what is a crime and what the punishment is. Procedural law tells us how the State may find, try and punish a person without becoming a criminal itself. The Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 came into force on 1 April 1974, replacing the 1898 Code, on the recommendations of the 41st Law Commission Report. From 1 July 2024 it is replaced by the **Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (BNSS)**, which keeps the structure but renumbers most sections; this guide gives the CrPC number first and the BNSS number beside it, because the exam is set on the Code but the courts now apply the Sanhita."));
c.push(P("**Two models.** In the **inquisitorial** system (France, Germany) the judge investigates, questions witnesses and builds the case file. In the **adversarial / accusatorial** system (England, India) two sides present evidence before a neutral judge who does not investigate; the burden is on the prosecution and the accused is presumed innocent. India's Code is adversarial with inquisitorial touches — the Magistrate can order investigation under Sec 156(3), examine witnesses himself, and the judge may put questions under Sec 165 of the Evidence Act. The international minimum standards come from the Universal Declaration (Arts 9-11) and the ICCPR (Arts 9, 14): no arbitrary arrest, presumption of innocence, fair and public hearing, right to counsel, no double jeopardy."));
c.push(H3("Constitutional foundations"));
c.push(SEC("Article 14","Equality before law — the Code must treat like cases alike; special procedures need a rational basis (State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar, 1952)."));
c.push(SEC("Article 20","(1) no ex post facto punishment; (2) no double jeopardy — mirrored in Sec 300 CrPC; (3) no compulsion to be a witness against oneself — the root of Sec 161(2), 313 and the rule that confessions must be voluntary."));
c.push(SEC("Article 21","Life and personal liberty only by procedure established by law, and after Maneka Gandhi (1978) that procedure must be fair, just and reasonable. This one article has produced the right to speedy trial (Hussainara Khatoon), legal aid (Khatri, Sec 304), bail as the rule, and the D.K. Basu arrest guidelines."));
c.push(SEC("Article 22","Rights of the arrested: to be told the grounds, to consult a lawyer, and to be produced before a Magistrate within 24 hours — restated in Sec 50, 41D, 56-57 and 167."));
c.push(H3("Salient features of the 1973 Code"));
c.push(P("Separation of the judiciary from the executive by dividing Magistrates into **Judicial** Magistrates (under the High Court, Sec 11-19) and **Executive** Magistrates (under the State Government, Sec 20-23) — a mandate of Article 50. Abolition of jury trial. Anticipatory bail (Sec 438). Legal aid at State expense (Sec 304). Summary trials widened. Time limits on cognizance (Sec 468). Sec 125 maintenance extended to parents. Later amendments added plea bargaining (2005), victim's right of appeal and compensation (2008), and the 2013 changes after the Delhi gang-rape."));
c.push(H3("The machinery"));
c.push(P("**Police** (Police Act 1861; Sec 2(o) officer in charge; Sec 36 superior officers; Sec 156 power to investigate cognizable cases). **Prosecution** (Sec 24 Public Prosecutors appointed by the government; Sec 25 Assistant PPs; Sec 25A Directorate of Prosecution; Sec 301-302 conduct of prosecution). **Defence** (Sec 303 right to be defended by a pleader of choice; Sec 304 legal aid). **Courts** (Sec 6: High Court, Court of Session, Judicial Magistrate First Class / Metropolitan Magistrate, Judicial Magistrate Second Class, Executive Magistrates; Sec 26-29 which court tries what and sentences it may pass: a Sessions Judge may pass any sentence but death needs High Court confirmation, a JMFC up to three years and fine of ten thousand rupees, a JMSC up to one year and five thousand rupees). **Prisons** (Prisons Act 1894; Sec 417-420 execution of sentences of imprisonment)."));
c.push(H3("Definitions to memorise — Sec 2 (BNSS Sec 2)"));
c.push(SEC("Investigation 2(h)","All proceedings for collection of evidence by a police officer or any person authorised by a Magistrate (not a Magistrate himself). Steps: go to the spot, ascertain facts, find and arrest the offender, collect evidence, form opinion, file report — H.N. Rishbud v. State of Delhi (1955)."));
c.push(SEC("Inquiry 2(g)","Every inquiry other than a trial, conducted by a Magistrate or Court — e.g. Sec 202, 125, 145 proceedings."));
c.push(SEC("Trial","Not defined; the judicial proceeding that ends in conviction or acquittal. Begins with framing of charge in warrant cases."));
c.push(SEC("Cognizable 2(c) / Non-cognizable 2(l)","Cognizable: police may arrest without warrant and investigate without Magistrate's order (First Schedule). Non-cognizable: neither, without Sec 155(2) order."));
c.push(SEC("Bailable 2(a)","Bail is a right (Sec 436); non-bailable: bail is judicial discretion (Sec 437, 439)."));
c.push(SEC("Complaint 2(d)","An allegation made orally or in writing to a Magistrate that some person has committed an offence; it excludes a police report, but a police report in a non-cognizable case is deemed a complaint."));
c.push(SEC("Charge 2(b)","Includes any head of charge; the precise accusation the accused must answer."));
c.push(SEC("Summons case 2(w) / Warrant case 2(x)","Warrant case: offence punishable with death, life or more than two years; everything else is a summons case."));
c.push(SEC("Compoundable","Sec 320 lists offences the victim may settle; all others are non-compoundable."));
c.push(SEC("Discharge vs acquittal","Discharge (Sec 227, 239, 245) is before charge on the ground of no sufficient case; acquittal is after trial on evidence — only acquittal attracts Sec 300."));
c.push(SEC("Appeal / Revision / Reference","Appeal is a statutory right to a higher court on facts and law (Sec 372-394); revision is discretionary supervision of correctness and legality (Sec 397-401); reference is a lower court sending a question of constitutional validity to the High Court (Sec 395)."));
c.push(H3("FIR and the start of investigation — Sec 154-157 (BNSS 173-176)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 154:** information of a cognizable offence given orally is reduced to writing, read over, signed, entered in the station book; a free copy goes to the informant; if the officer refuses, the informant may send it in writing to the Superintendent (154(3)). After 2013, information about sexual offences from a woman is recorded by a woman officer, and a disabled victim's statement is video-recorded. BNSS 173 adds **e-FIR** (electronic information, to be signed within three days) and **zero FIR** at any police station regardless of jurisdiction. **Sec 155:** non-cognizable information is entered in the book and the informant sent to the Magistrate; investigation only with the Magistrate's order (155(2)); a case with one cognizable offence is treated as cognizable (155(4)). **Sec 156:** power to investigate cognizable cases without order; 156(3) Magistrate may order investigation — a pre-cognizance power. **Sec 157:** the officer sends a report to the Magistrate forthwith and proceeds to the spot; he may decline to investigate if the case is not serious or there is no sufficient ground, recording reasons and informing the informant."));
c.push(P("**Evidentiary value of the FIR:** it is not substantive evidence; it corroborates the informant under Sec 157 IEA or contradicts him under Sec 145; if made by the accused it may be an admission (Aghnoo Nagesia) but not a confession of the crime; delay in lodging must be explained (Thulia Kali, 1972) but does not by itself destroy the case."));
c.push(CASE("Lalita Kumari v. Govt. of U.P. (2014) 2 SCC 1","Registration of FIR under Sec 154 is mandatory when information discloses a cognizable offence; preliminary enquiry is permitted only in listed categories, only to ascertain whether a cognizable offence is disclosed, and must close within seven days (later extended to fifteen)."));
c.push(CASE("State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal (1992)","Seven categories in which the High Court may quash an FIR under Sec 482 — including where the allegations, taken at face value, do not disclose any offence."));
c.push(CASE("H.N. Rishbud v. State of Delhi (1955)","Defined the stages of investigation; a defect in investigation does not vitiate cognizance unless it causes miscarriage of justice."));
c.push(CASE("Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)","'Procedure established by law' in Art 21 must be fair, just and reasonable — the constitutional yardstick for every provision of the Code."));
c.push(DIG("Every State now has an online FIR portal and BNSS 173 makes e-FIR statutory. The CCTNS network links police stations so a zero FIR registered in Hyderabad is transferred electronically to the station having jurisdiction. Under BNSS 176 the investigating officer must videograph the search and seizure and forensic experts must visit the scene for offences punishable with seven years or more. The Lalita Kumari general-diary entry is now often a computerised station diary — an 'electronic record' that itself needs a Sec 63 BSA certificate if produced in court."));
c.push(TIP("Part A favourites: 'Cognizable and non-cognizable offence', 'Complaint', 'FIR', 'Judicial and Executive Magistrate' — three marks each: definition, section, one line of difference. Part B: 'Is registration of FIR mandatory? Discuss with reference to Lalita Kumari' — write Sec 154 text, the split among High Courts, the five-judge answer, the exceptions and the seven-day rule, then the remedy under 154(3) and 156(3). Part C problem: an officer refuses to register an FIR because he thinks the complaint is false — answer: refusal is illegal, truth is for investigation, remedies are 154(3), 156(3), and a writ."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — Eleven Rules in the Lock-up: D.K. Basu and Arnesh Kumar"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Arrest with and without warrant (Sec 41-60, 70-80) · Notice instead of arrest (Sec 41A) · Rights of the arrested person (Sec 50, 50A, 54-57, 75-76, 303-304, Art 22) · Preventive powers of the police (Sec 149-153)"));
c.push(STORY("Calcutta, 1986. D.K. Basu, chairman of a legal aid organisation, reads newspaper reports of deaths in police lock-ups and writes a letter to the Chief Justice of India. The letter is treated as a writ petition. For ten years the case grows as more States are added, until in December 1996 Justice Kuldip Singh lays down eleven requirements that every arresting officer in India must follow: a visible name tag, an arrest memo signed by a witness and the arrestee, a friend or relative informed, the arrest entered in a diary, a medical examination on request and every forty-eight hours, copies of the memo to the Magistrate, a lawyer allowed during interrogation, and a police control room notice board. The Court said these were not mere directions; breach would be contempt and departmental action. Eighteen years later, in Bihar, a man named Arnesh Kumar was arrested on his wife's dowry complaint under Section 498A, the most-used arrest provision in the country. The Supreme Court, alarmed that arrest had become routine and bail an ordeal, ordered in 2014 that for offences punishable up to seven years the police must first issue a notice under Section 41A and may arrest only after recording, in writing, why the conditions of Section 41 are satisfied — and the Magistrate must check those reasons before authorising detention. Two petitions, twenty years apart, turned the power of arrest from a reflex into a reasoned act."));
c.push(H3("Arrest without warrant — Sec 41 (BNSS 35)"));
c.push(P("After the 2008 amendment, for a cognizable offence punishable up to seven years the police may arrest without warrant only if (a) the person has committed the offence in the officer's presence, or (b) there is a reasonable complaint / credible information / reasonable suspicion **and** the officer is satisfied that arrest is necessary to prevent further offence, for proper investigation, to prevent tampering with evidence, to prevent threats to witnesses, or to secure presence in court — and the reasons must be recorded (41(1)(b)(ii)). For offences punishable with more than seven years, credible information plus reasonable suspicion suffices (41(1)(ba)). Other grounds: proclaimed offender, possession of stolen property, obstructing a police officer, deserter, requisition from another station. **Sec 41A:** where arrest is not required, issue a notice to appear; compliance bars arrest unless reasons are recorded. **Sec 41B:** name tag, arrest memo, relative informed. **Sec 41C:** control room. **Sec 41D:** right to meet an advocate during interrogation (not throughout). BNSS 35(7) adds: no arrest of a person above sixty or infirm for offences up to three years without prior permission of a DSP."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 42","Arrest for non-cognizable offence when the person refuses to give name and address."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 43","Arrest by a private person of one committing a non-bailable cognizable offence in his presence; hand over to police without delay."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 44","Arrest by a Magistrate for an offence in his presence."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 46","How arrest is made — touch or confine the body unless there is submission; a woman is not touched by a male officer unless the circumstances require, and not arrested after sunset and before sunrise without a lady officer and a Magistrate's permission (46(4)). Force may be used if the person resists, but death may be caused only for offences punishable with death or life (46(3))."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 47-49","Entry into a place to arrest, breaking open doors, no more restraint than necessary."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 50, 50A","Grounds of arrest and right to bail must be communicated; a relative or friend must be informed and the Magistrate must satisfy himself that this was done. Prabir Purkayastha v. State (2024): grounds must be given in writing; a copy not supplied vitiates the arrest."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 51-53","Search of the arrested person; seizure of offensive weapons; medical examination by a registered practitioner at the request of police (53), including DNA and other tests; 53A for rape accused; 54 examination at the request of the arrested person."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 55A","Duty of the person having custody to take reasonable care of health and safety (inserted 2008 after Basu)."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 56-57","Person arrested to be taken before a Magistrate or officer in charge without unnecessary delay, and not detained beyond twenty-four hours excluding journey time (Art 22(2))."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 58-60","Report of arrests to the District Magistrate; discharge only on bond, bail or Magistrate's order; power to pursue and retake an escapee."));
c.push(H3("Arrest with warrant — Sec 70-81 (BNSS 72-83)"));
c.push(P("A warrant is in writing, signed by the presiding officer, sealed, names the person, and stays in force until executed or cancelled (Sec 70). It may be **bailable** — endorsed with a direction to release on security (Sec 71). It is directed to police officers (Sec 72), may be directed to any person for an escaped convict or proclaimed offender (Sec 73), and must be notified in substance to the arrestee and shown on demand (Sec 75). The person is produced without unnecessary delay, within twenty-four hours (Sec 76). A warrant may be executed anywhere in India (Sec 77) and forwarded to another district (Sec 78-79); arrest outside the issuing district requires production before the local Magistrate (Sec 80-81)."));
c.push(H3("Preventive powers — Sec 149-153 (BNSS 168-172)"));
c.push(P("A police officer may interpose to prevent a cognizable offence (149), must report a design to commit one (150), may arrest without warrant a person designing a cognizable offence if it cannot otherwise be prevented, detaining him not more than twenty-four hours (151 — the section used for round-ups before processions), may prevent injury to public property (152), and may inspect weights and measures (153). Sec 151 is constitutionally tolerated only because of the twenty-four-hour limit; Ahmed Noormohmed Bhatti v. State of Gujarat (2005) upheld it."));
c.push(H3("Rights of the arrested person — the constitutional list"));
c.push(P("Article 22(1)-(2) plus the Code: to know the grounds (50, 41B); to bail in bailable cases (50(2)); to have a relative informed (50A); production within twenty-four hours (57, 76); medical examination (54); a lawyer of choice (303, Art 22(1)) and during interrogation (41D); free legal aid where he cannot afford one (304; Khatri v. State of Bihar, 1981; Hussainara Khatoon; Suk Das v. Arunachal Pradesh, 1986 — conviction set aside for failure to provide counsel); silence (Art 20(3), Nandini Satpathy); no handcuffing as a routine (Prem Shankar Shukla v. Delhi Administration, 1980); no torture (Basu; Art 21); compensation for unlawful arrest (Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar, 1983; Nilabati Behera, 1993). Arrest of women: Sec 46(4), Sheela Barse v. State of Maharashtra (1983) — separate lock-ups and female constables."));
c.push(CASE("D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997) 1 SCC 416","Eleven arrest and detention requirements under Art 21 and 22, binding on all States; failure is contempt of court. Most were codified by the 2008 amendment as Sec 41B, 41C, 41D, 50A, 55A."));
c.push(CASE("Joginder Kumar v. State of U.P. (1994)","Arrest is not to be made merely because it is lawful; the officer must justify it. Right to have a friend or relative informed."));
c.push(CASE("Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014) 8 SCC 273","For offences punishable up to seven years, police must satisfy Sec 41 and record reasons; issue notice under 41A instead of arresting; Magistrate must record satisfaction before authorising detention; non-compliance invites departmental action and contempt. Reaffirmed in Satender Kumar Antil (2022)."));
c.push(CASE("Prabir Purkayastha v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2024)","Written grounds of arrest must be furnished to the arrestee — extended from PMLA (Pankaj Bansal) to UAPA and, by reasoning, to all arrests; oral communication is insufficient."));
c.push(DIG("Arrest memos are now generated on tablets and uploaded to CCTNS; BNSS 37 requires every district and police station to display arrest information digitally. Body-worn cameras and lock-up CCTV (Paramvir Singh Saini v. Baljit Singh, 2020 — CCTV mandatory in every police station with audio and eighteen months' storage) make the D.K. Basu memo verifiable: the footage is an electronic record provable under Sec 63 BSA. A 41A notice may be served electronically under BNSS 35(3)."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Anticipatory bail vs bail after arrest', 'Rights of an arrested person', 'Section 41A notice' — three marks: section plus case. Part B: 'Discuss the rights of an arrested person under the Constitution and the Code' — structure as before arrest (41, 41A), at arrest (46, 50, 50A, 41B), in custody (54, 55A, 57, 41D, 303, 304), plus Basu and Arnesh Kumar; end with remedies (habeas corpus, compensation, departmental action). Part C: police arrest a 498A accused without notice and the Magistrate remands mechanically — apply Arnesh Kumar, say the arrest and remand are illegal, mention 41A, 167 and departmental action."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — The Right to Stay Silent: Nandini Satpathy and the Investigation File"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Police questioning of witnesses and accused (Sec 160-161) · Use of statements to police (Sec 162 CrPC with Sec 145, 157 IEA) · Recording of confessions and statements by a Magistrate (Sec 164) · Remand (Sec 167) · Case diary (Sec 172) · Police report (Sec 173) · Inquest (Sec 174-176)"));
c.push(STORY("Cuttack, 1977. Nandini Satpathy has just stepped down as Chief Minister of Orissa. A vigilance case is registered against her for disproportionate assets. The Deputy Superintendent of Police, P.L. Dani, sends her a long written questionnaire and summons her to the police station under Section 160. She refuses to answer, saying the questions would incriminate her. She is prosecuted under Section 179 of the Penal Code for refusing to answer a public servant. The case reaches Justice Krishna Iyer, who has to reconcile two texts: Section 161(2), which says a person is bound to answer truthfully all questions except those that tend to incriminate him, and Article 20(3), which says no accused person shall be compelled to be a witness against himself. The Court held that 'accused' includes a suspect who may become an accused; the protection covers answers that would furnish a link in the chain of evidence; the police cannot summon a woman to the station at all (Sec 160 proviso); and a lawyer's presence at questioning is a fair-procedure guarantee. Nandini Satpathy walked out of the police station with the right to silence intact — and every investigating officer's questionnaire has carried that limit since."));
c.push(H3("Questioning — Sec 160-162 (BNSS 179-181)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 160:** the officer may require by written order the attendance of any person acquainted with the facts, but a male under fifteen or above sixty-five, a woman, or a mentally or physically disabled person shall not be required to attend anywhere other than their residence. **Sec 161:** oral examination; the person is bound to answer truly except self-incriminating questions; the statement may be reduced to writing (separate record for each) and may be audio-video recorded; a woman victim of sexual offences is examined by a woman officer. **Sec 162:** no statement to a police officer during investigation shall be signed, and it cannot be used for any purpose at trial **except** — by the accused to contradict the witness under Sec 145 IEA, or by the prosecution, with the court's permission, to contradict; and it may be used to re-examine to explain the contradiction. Sec 162 does not apply to dying declarations (Sec 32(1) IEA) or to discovery statements under Sec 27 IEA. The purpose is to prevent police from manufacturing evidence; the statement is therefore neither corroboration under Sec 157 IEA nor substantive evidence (Tahsildar Singh v. State of U.P., 1959 — only what is actually recorded, or an omission that amounts to a contradiction, may be put to the witness)."));
c.push(H3("Confessions and statements before a Magistrate — Sec 164 (BNSS 183)"));
c.push(P("Any Metropolitan or Judicial Magistrate may record a confession or statement during investigation, before the inquiry or trial begins, and it may be audio-video recorded in the presence of the accused's advocate. Before recording a confession the Magistrate must explain that the person is not bound to confess and that it may be used against him, and must be satisfied it is voluntary — and no police officer may record it. If the person is unwilling, he is not remanded to police custody (164(3)). The confession is signed by the maker and a memorandum is added (164(4)). Statements of victims of sexual offences are recorded as soon as the offence comes to notice, by a woman Magistrate where possible (164(5A), 2013 amendment). A Sec 164 statement is not substantive evidence; it corroborates or contradicts the witness; a Sec 164 confession is substantive but requires corroboration if retracted. Safeguards: the Magistrate must give time for reflection (usually twenty-four hours in judicial custody) — Kashmira Singh; Shivappa v. State of Karnataka (1995) held that failure to follow the safeguards renders the confession unusable."));
c.push(H3("Remand — Sec 167 (BNSS 187)"));
c.push(P("When investigation cannot finish within twenty-four hours, the accused is sent to the nearest Magistrate with the case diary entries. The Magistrate may authorise detention in police or judicial custody for up to fifteen days in total; beyond that only judicial custody, up to ninety days for offences punishable with death, life or ten years or more, and sixty days for other offences. If the charge-sheet is not filed within that period the accused is entitled to **default bail** (167(2) proviso) — an indefeasible right if he applies before the charge-sheet arrives (Sanjay Dutt, 1994; Rakesh Kumar Paul v. State of Assam, 2017 — 'ten years or more' means punishable with a minimum of ten years; M. Ravindran v. Intelligence Officer, 2020). Police custody was limited to the first fifteen days (CBI v. Anupam J. Kulkarni, 1992); BNSS 187(2) now allows the fifteen days of police custody to be taken in parts during the first forty or sixty days — the most debated change in the new Code. A Magistrate cannot remand mechanically; he must apply his mind (Arnesh Kumar)."));
c.push(H3("Diary, report and inquest — Sec 172-176 (BNSS 192-196)"));
c.push(SEC("Sec 172 case diary","Day-to-day record of the investigation, in a bound volume with numbered pages (2008). The court may call for it to aid the inquiry, but it is not evidence; the accused cannot see it unless the officer uses it to refresh memory or the court uses it to contradict him (Sec 161 and 145 IEA)."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 173 police report","Investigation is completed without unnecessary delay (rape of a child within two months — 173(1A)); the charge-sheet (or final report) is forwarded to the Magistrate with the names of parties, witnesses, whether the accused is in custody, and in sexual offence cases the medical report; the accused gets copies under Sec 207. Further investigation is permitted after the report (173(8)) — Vinay Tyagi v. Irshad Ali (2013): 'further' investigation is a continuation, 'fresh' or 're' investigation needs the superior court's order. BNSS 193 sets a ninety-day outer limit for supplementary reports and requires informing the victim of progress within ninety days."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 174-176 inquest","On unnatural death the officer in charge, with two respectable neighbours, examines the body and draws an inquest report of apparent cause of death; it is sent to the District Magistrate. Where a woman dies within seven years of marriage in suspicious circumstances, or the relatives request it, the body is sent for post-mortem (174(3)). The inquest report is not substantive evidence and need not mention the accused's name (Podda Narayana v. State of A.P., 1975). Sec 176: Magisterial inquiry — mandatory judicial magisterial inquiry for deaths in police custody, rape in custody, or disappearance in custody (176(1A), 2005 amendment)."));
c.push(CASE("Nandini Satpathy v. P.L. Dani (1978) 2 SCC 424","'Accused' in Art 20(3) includes a suspect; the privilege extends to answers that furnish a link in the chain of incrimination; a woman cannot be summoned to the police station; the presence of a lawyer during interrogation is a fair-procedure safeguard."));
c.push(CASE("Tahsildar Singh v. State of U.P. (1959)","Only statements actually recorded under Sec 161 may be used to contradict under Sec 145 IEA; an omission is a contradiction only if it is significant enough to be inconsistent with the deposition."));
c.push(CASE("CBI v. Anupam J. Kulkarni (1992)","Police custody only within the first fifteen days of remand; thereafter judicial custody alone (now modified by BNSS 187)."));
c.push(CASE("Rakesh Kumar Paul v. State of Assam (2017)","Default bail under Sec 167(2): the ninety-day limit applies only where the offence carries a minimum of ten years; otherwise sixty days; the right accrues the moment the period ends if the accused applies."));
c.push(DIG("Sec 161(3) and 164(1) already allow audio-video recording; BNSS makes it the norm for statements of witnesses in serious cases and for search and seizure (BNSS 105). A video-recorded Sec 164 statement is an electronic record, so the court needs the Sec 63 BSA certificate from the person in charge of the recording device. Case diaries are increasingly maintained on e-Prosecution portals; the accused still cannot inspect them, but courts can call the electronic diary under Sec 172(2). Remand hearings can be held through video conferencing (Sec 167(2)(b) proviso, 2008; BNSS 187 explicitly)."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Case diary', 'Default bail', 'Inquest', 'Evidentiary value of a Sec 161 statement' — each is a classic three-marker. Part B: 'Explain the provisions relating to the recording of confessions and statements by Magistrates under Sec 164' — write who may record, the warning, voluntariness, audio-video recording, the 2013 sexual-offence provision, the evidentiary value and the Shivappa safeguards. Part C: a suspect is kept in police custody beyond fifteen days, or charge-sheet not filed within sixty days — apply Sec 167, Anupam Kulkarni, Rakesh Kumar Paul, and the BNSS change."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Midnight Search: Pooran Mal and the Proclaimed Offender"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Search and seizure (Sec 51, 93-103, 165-166B) · Proclamation and attachment (Sec 82-89) · What happens to illegally obtained evidence"));
c.push(STORY("Delhi, 1970s. Income-tax officers, armed with a warrant under Section 132 of the Income-tax Act, raid the business premises of Pooran Mal and seize account books, cash and jewellery. Pooran Mal moves the Supreme Court: the search, he says, violated his fundamental rights and the seized material must be returned and cannot be used against him. A Constitution Bench in 1974 gave the answer that still governs every police search in India: even if a search is illegal, the evidence found is not excluded. Indian law, unlike the American Fourth Amendment rule, has no doctrine of the 'fruit of the poisonous tree'; the test of admissibility is relevancy, and the remedy for an illegal search is action against the officer, not suppression of what he found. That answer makes the procedural safeguards of the Code — witnesses to the search, a seizure list, a copy to the occupant — matter more, not less, because they are what a court will use to weigh the credibility of the recovery. The same chapter of the Code deals with the man who cannot be found at all: the proclaimed offender, whose property the court attaches so that absence itself becomes expensive."));
c.push(H3("Processes to compel production — Sec 91-93 (BNSS 94-96)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 91:** a court or officer in charge may issue a summons or written order to produce a document or thing; it does not apply to the accused, who cannot be compelled to produce incriminating documents (State of Gujarat v. Shyamlal Mohanlal Choksi, 1965; Art 20(3)). **Sec 92:** postal and telegraph authorities may be directed to deliver documents. **Sec 93:** a **search warrant** may issue where a Sec 91 summons would not be obeyed, where the document is not known to be in any person's possession, or for a general search of a place; it may be restricted to a part of the premises. A search warrant for a document in the custody of the postal authority may be issued only by a District Magistrate or Chief Judicial Magistrate."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 94-96","Search of a place suspected to contain stolen property, forged documents, counterfeit coin or obscene material; forfeiture of publications and search for them."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 97-98","Search for a person wrongfully confined; restoration of an abducted woman or girl under eighteen."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 99-101","Warrants must state the place with clarity; the Sec 38, 70, 72, 74, 77-79 rules of execution apply; they may be executed outside the local jurisdiction."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 100","The general rules of search: the person in charge must allow free ingress; the searching officer may break open doors; a person about to leave may be searched (100(3)); **two or more independent and respectable inhabitants of the locality** must attend as witnesses (100(4)); a seizure list is prepared, signed by the witnesses, and a copy given to the occupant (100(5)-(6)); the occupant may attend the search (100(6)); the witnesses need not attend court unless summoned (100(5)). Non-compliance is an irregularity affecting weight, not admissibility (State of Punjab v. Balbir Singh, 1994; Sunder Singh v. State of U.P., 1956)."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 102","A police officer may seize property alleged or suspected to be stolen or found in circumstances creating suspicion; he reports the seizure to the Magistrate. A bank account can be 'property' (State of Maharashtra v. Tapas D. Neogy, 1999). BNSS 106 adds a requirement of forensic collection and videography for seizures in serious cases and reporting within forty-eight hours."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 103","A Magistrate may direct a search in his presence."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 51","Search of the arrested person; articles other than necessary wearing apparel may be seized with a receipt; a woman is searched by a woman with strict decency."));
c.push(H3("Search by police officer during investigation — Sec 165-166B (BNSS 185-186)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 165:** where an officer in charge has reasonable grounds to believe that something necessary for the investigation is in a place within his jurisdiction and cannot be obtained without undue delay, he may search after recording in writing the grounds of his belief and the thing searched for; he conducts it personally if practicable, else deputes a subordinate with a written order; copies of the record go to the Magistrate and, on application, to the occupant. Sec 100 applies to such searches. **Sec 166:** an officer may require another station's officer to search a place in that station's jurisdiction, or search himself if delay would defeat the purpose. **Sec 166A-166B:** letters rogatory — a court may issue a letter of request to a foreign court for investigation abroad, and act on a foreign request, through the Central Government (mutual legal assistance)."));
c.push(H3("Proclamation and attachment — Sec 82-89 (BNSS 84-91)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 82:** where a court believes that a person against whom a warrant has been issued has absconded or is concealing himself so that the warrant cannot be executed, it publishes a written proclamation requiring him to appear at a specified place and time not less than thirty days later; publication is by reading in the town, affixing at his house and on the court notice-board, and optionally in a newspaper. For serious listed offences (murder, dacoity, kidnapping, rape and others), the court may on non-appearance declare him a **proclaimed offender** (82(4), 2005 amendment) — a status that allows arrest by any person (Sec 43), attracts the offence under Sec 174A IPC (BNS 209), and bars anticipatory bail (Lavesh v. State, 2012). **Sec 83:** attachment of the person's movable and immovable property, even simultaneously with the proclamation if he is about to dispose of it; **Sec 84:** claims and objections by third parties within six months; **Sec 85:** release if he appears within the time, otherwise the property is at the disposal of the State Government, sold after six months; **Sec 86:** appeal against rejection of a claim; **Sec 87-89:** a warrant may issue in lieu of, or in addition to, a summons, and the court may take a bond for appearance and arrest on breach. BNSS 84(4) widens the proclaimed-offender category to all offences punishable with ten years or more, and BNSS 356 permits trial in absentia of a proclaimed offender — a genuine novelty."));
c.push(CASE("Pooran Mal v. Director of Inspection (1974) 1 SCC 345","Evidence obtained by an illegal search is not inadmissible; India follows the relevancy test, not an exclusionary rule. Followed in State of M.P. v. Paltan Mallah (2005) and Umesh Kumar v. State of A.P. (2013)."));
c.push(CASE("State of Punjab v. Balbir Singh (1994)","Sec 100 and 165 requirements are directory; non-compliance affects the weight of the recovery evidence, but mandatory provisions of special Acts (NDPS Sec 42, 50) stand on a different footing."));
c.push(CASE("State of Maharashtra v. Tapas D. Neogy (1999)","A bank account is 'property' that a police officer may seize (freeze) under Sec 102."));
c.push(CASE("Lavesh v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2012)","A person declared a proclaimed offender under Sec 82 is not entitled to anticipatory bail."));
c.push(DIG("Searches of phones and laptops are 'searches of a place' under Sec 165 in practice, though the Code was written for rooms and boxes; BNSS 105 makes videography of search and seizure and the preparation of the list mandatory, with the recording forwarded to the Magistrate. Cloud data outside India needs a letter rogatory (166A) or an MLAT request; the CLOUD Act arrangements are the modern form. Seizure of digital devices raises the Pooran Mal question again — Virendra Khanna v. State of Karnataka (2021) held that a court may direct an accused to unlock a phone, but the data so obtained is tested for relevancy and Art 20(3) in the same way as a search."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Proclaimed offender', 'Search warrant', 'Letter rogatory' — three marks each. Part B: 'Explain the provisions relating to search and seizure and the consequences of an illegal search' — write Sec 93 (when a warrant issues), Sec 100 (how a search is done: witnesses, list, copy), Sec 165 (search without warrant during investigation: recorded reasons), then Pooran Mal and Balbir Singh on the effect of irregularity. Part C: a search is made without independent witnesses and the seizure list is prepared later at the station — answer that the recovery is admissible but its weight is reduced; the court will look for corroboration."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — The Chief Minister's Complaint: Bhajan Lal and the Gates of the Court"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Jurisdiction of criminal courts (Sec 177-189) · Cognizance (Sec 190-194) · Bars on cognizance (Sec 195-199) · Complaints to Magistrates (Sec 200-203) · Commencement of proceedings (Sec 204-210) · Quashing under Sec 482"));
c.push(STORY("Haryana, November 1987. Dharam Pal, a political rival, files a complaint with the Superintendent of Police alleging that the former Chief Minister Bhajan Lal has amassed properties worth crores in the names of relatives. An FIR is registered under the Prevention of Corruption Act and Section 161 IPC. Bhajan Lal moves the High Court, which quashes the FIR on the ground that the allegations are politically motivated. The State appeals. In 1992 the Supreme Court restored the investigation but, in doing so, wrote the seven-point catalogue that every High Court now recites before quashing an FIR under Section 482: where the allegations even if true disclose no offence; where the FIR discloses no cognizable offence justifying investigation without a Magistrate's order; where the uncontroverted allegations disclose no offence; where the allegations disclose a non-cognizable offence and no Magistrate has ordered investigation; where the allegations are so absurd that no prudent person could reach the conclusion of an offence; where there is an express legal bar; and where the proceeding is manifestly mala fide or a vehicle for private vengeance. The Court warned that this power is to be used sparingly, in the rarest of rare cases, and not to stifle a legitimate prosecution. Bhajan Lal's case is thus about the gates of the criminal court: which court may hear a case, how it takes cognizance, when the law shuts the gate, and when a superior court may open it or slam it."));
c.push(H3("Which court — Sec 177-189 (BNSS 197-209)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 177**, the ordinary rule: an offence is inquired into and tried by the court within whose local jurisdiction it was committed. The exceptions widen the choice: **178** where it is uncertain in which of several areas the offence was committed, or it was committed partly in one and partly in another, or it is a continuing offence, or consists of several acts in different areas — any of those courts; **179** the court where the act was done or where its consequence ensued; **180** where an act is an offence because of its relation to another offence — either court; **181** special rules for thugs, dacoity, escape from custody, kidnapping (place of abduction or where the person was conveyed), theft and criminal breach of trust (where committed or where the property was received or retained), and for misappropriation of stolen property; **182** offences by letters or electronic messages — where sent or received; bigamy — where the offender last resided with the spouse; **183** offences in the course of a journey or voyage; **184** offences triable together (Sec 219-221, 223) — any court competent for one; **185** the State Government may order a case to be tried in a different sessions division; **186** the High Court decides between courts where it is doubtful; **187** a Magistrate may issue process for an offence committed outside his jurisdiction; **188** offences committed outside India by a citizen, or by anyone on an Indian-registered ship or aircraft, may be tried in India with the Central Government's sanction; **189** receipt of evidence relating to offences committed outside India."));
c.push(H3("Cognizance — Sec 190-194 (BNSS 210-214)"));
c.push(P("'Cognizance' is not defined; it means the Magistrate applying his judicial mind to the suspected commission of an offence with a view to proceeding (R.R. Chari v. State of U.P., 1951; Darshan Singh Ram Kishan v. State of Maharashtra, 1971). **Sec 190:** any Magistrate of the first class, or a second-class Magistrate empowered, may take cognizance (a) on a complaint, (b) on a police report, (c) on information from any person other than a police officer, or on his own knowledge. **Sec 191:** where cognizance is taken under (c) the accused may insist on trial by another Magistrate. **Sec 192:** the Chief Judicial Magistrate may transfer the case to a subordinate Magistrate. **Sec 193:** a Court of Session cannot take cognizance as a court of original jurisdiction unless the case is committed to it by a Magistrate (Sec 209) — except where a statute provides otherwise. **Sec 194:** Additional and Assistant Sessions Judges try cases made over to them. Cognizance is of the offence, not of the offender: a court may summon persons not named in the charge-sheet (Sec 319; Hardeep Singh v. State of Punjab, 2014)."));
c.push(H3("When the gate is shut — Sec 195-199 (BNSS 215-222)"));
c.push(SEC("Sec 195","No cognizance of contempts of lawful authority of public servants (IPC 172-188) except on complaint of the public servant; no cognizance of offences against public justice (193-196, 199-200, 205-211, 228) or forgery of documents produced in court (463, 471, 475, 476) when committed in relation to a court proceeding except on the written complaint of that court (Sec 340 procedure) — Iqbal Singh Marwah v. Meenakshi Marwah (2005): the bar applies only to documents forged after they were produced in court, not before."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 196","Offences against the State (IPC Chapter VI), promoting enmity (153A, 295A), criminal conspiracy to commit certain offences — only with previous sanction of the Central or State Government."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 197","Judges, Magistrates and public servants not removable without government sanction cannot be prosecuted for acts done 'while acting or purporting to act in discharge of official duty' without sanction — the act must have a reasonable connection with official duty (Matajog Dobey v. H.C. Bhari, 1955; P.K. Pradhan v. State of Sikkim, 2001); demanding a bribe is never part of official duty (State of Kerala v. Padmanabhan Nair, 1999)."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 198","Offences against marriage (IPC Chapter XX: bigamy, adultery — now struck down in Joseph Shine, 2018 — enticing a married woman) only on complaint of the person aggrieved; rape by a husband of a wife under eighteen requires her complaint within a year (198(6)); 198A: cruelty under 498A on police report or complaint by the woman or her relatives; 198B (BNSS 222): sexual intercourse by husband during separation."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 199","Defamation only on complaint of the person aggrieved; defamation of the President, Governor, Ministers or public servants in respect of public functions — a Public Prosecutor may complain before a Court of Session with the government's sanction (Subramanian Swamy v. Union of India, 2016 upheld criminal defamation)."));
c.push(H3("Complaints to Magistrates — Sec 200-203 (BNSS 223-226)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 200:** the Magistrate taking cognizance on a complaint examines the complainant and witnesses on oath, records the substance and obtains their signatures; a public servant's complaint in official capacity, or a court's complaint, need not be so examined; BNSS 223 adds that the accused must be heard before cognizance is taken on a complaint. **Sec 201:** a Magistrate not competent returns the complaint for presentation to the proper court. **Sec 202:** he may postpone the issue of process and inquire himself or direct investigation by police or another person to decide whether there is sufficient ground for proceeding; where the accused lives outside his jurisdiction the inquiry is mandatory (2005 amendment — Vijay Dhanuka v. Najima Mamtaj, 2014); where the offence is exclusively triable by a Court of Session he must call all the complainant's witnesses; investigation by police cannot be ordered where the offence is exclusively triable by Sessions or where the complainant's witnesses have not been examined. **Sec 203:** dismissal of the complaint if there is no sufficient ground, with brief reasons. Note the two roads from Sec 156(3) and 202: a Sec 156(3) order is pre-cognizance and leads to an FIR and a police report; a Sec 202 order is post-cognizance and is merely to help the Magistrate decide on process (Devarapalli Lakshminarayana Reddy v. Narayana Reddy, 1976)."));
c.push(H3("Commencement of proceedings — Sec 204-210 (BNSS 227-233)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 204:** if there is sufficient ground the Magistrate issues a summons (summons case) or a warrant or summons (warrant case); no process issues until the list of prosecution witnesses is filed and, on a complaint in writing, a copy accompanies the summons; process fees must be paid. Issuing process is a serious matter requiring application of mind (Pepsi Foods v. Special Judicial Magistrate, 1998; Mehmood Ul Rehman v. Khazir Mohammad Tunda, 2015) and there is no power to review or recall it (Adalat Prasad v. Rooplal Jindal, 2004 — the remedy is Sec 482). **Sec 205:** dispensing with personal attendance. **Sec 206:** special summons for petty offences (fine up to one thousand rupees) allowing plea of guilty by post. **Sec 207-208:** free copies of the police report, FIR, statements and documents to the accused; in complaint cases triable by Sessions, copies of the statements under 200 and 202. **Sec 209:** commitment to the Court of Session when the offence is exclusively triable by it — the Magistrate does not weigh evidence, remands the accused and sends the record. **Sec 210:** where a complaint case and a police investigation concern the same offence, the Magistrate stays the complaint proceeding until the police report, then tries them together."));
c.push(CASE("State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal 1992 Supp (1) SCC 335","Seven illustrative categories for quashing an FIR or complaint under Sec 482 / Art 226; power to be exercised sparingly and not to stifle a legitimate prosecution at the threshold."));
c.push(CASE("Devarapalli Lakshminarayana Reddy v. V. Narayana Reddy (1976)","Distinguishes Sec 156(3) (pre-cognizance, police investigate and report) from Sec 202 (post-cognizance, investigation only to decide whether to issue process)."));
c.push(CASE("Adalat Prasad v. Rooplal Jindal (2004)","A Magistrate has no power to recall or review an order issuing process; the accused must approach the High Court under Sec 482 (overruling K.M. Mathew)."));
c.push(CASE("Matajog Dobey v. H.C. Bhari (1955)","Sanction under Sec 197 is needed only where the act complained of is reasonably connected with the discharge of official duty."));
c.push(DIG("Complaints under Sec 200 are increasingly filed through e-filing portals and examined via video conferencing (Sec 200 read with the e-Courts rules; BNSS 223 permits electronic filing and summons). Summons under BNSS 63-64 may be served electronically, with the electronic record of service treated as proof. Jurisdiction under Sec 182 for offences by 'electronic messages' — cheating by phishing mail, cyber-stalking — lies where the message was sent or received, which is why a cyber-crime cell in Hyderabad can register a case against a caller in another State."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Cognizance', 'Sec 156(3) vs Sec 202', 'Sanction for prosecution of public servants', 'Sec 482 inherent powers'. Part B: 'Discuss the general principles of jurisdiction of criminal courts and the exceptions' — Sec 177 then 178-188 in order, one example each; or 'Explain the procedure on a complaint from Sec 200 to 204' — examination, 202 inquiry, 203 dismissal, 204 process, with Pepsi Foods and Adalat Prasad. Part C: a Magistrate issues process without examining the complainant, or a public servant is prosecuted without sanction — say the order is illegal and give the remedy."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — Bail, Not Jail: Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and the Two Thousand Undertrials"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The philosophy of bail · Bailable offences (Sec 436, 436A) · Non-bailable offences (Sec 437, 439) · Anticipatory bail (Sec 438) · Bonds, sureties, cancellation and forfeiture (Sec 440-450)"));
c.push(STORY("Punjab, 1978. Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia, a former Minister of Irrigation, expects to be arrested in a corruption case. He applies to the High Court under the new Section 438 for an order that, if arrested, he shall be released on bail. The High Court grants nothing, and a Full Bench lays down eight strict conditions for anticipatory bail — the applicant must show a special case, the power is to be used sparingly, no blanket orders, and so on. In 1980 a Constitution Bench of the Supreme Court under Chief Justice Chandrachud swept those conditions away. Section 438, it said, was a new provision enacted on the recommendation of the 41st Law Commission because influential people were getting rivals arrested to disgrace them; Parliament had deliberately left the discretion wide, and the courts must not narrow it with judge-made conditions. Anticipatory bail need not be limited in time, need not be refused merely because the offence is serious, and the applicant need not first go to the Sessions Court. The judgment read Section 438 with Article 21: the presumption of innocence means liberty is the rule. A year earlier the same Court, in Hussainara Khatoon, had discovered thousands of undertrials in Bihar jails who had been waiting longer than the maximum sentence for their alleged crimes — the fact that produced Section 436A in 2005. Bail, the Court kept saying, is the rule; jail is the exception."));
c.push(H3("Bailable offences — Sec 436, 436A (BNSS 478-479)"));
c.push(P("In a bailable offence bail is a matter of right: the officer in charge or the court **shall** release the person on bail, or on his own bond without sureties if he is indigent — and a person who cannot furnish bail within a week of arrest is presumed indigent (436(1) proviso, 2005). If he breaks the conditions of the bond, the court may refuse bail on a later appearance (436(2)). **Sec 436A:** an undertrial who has been detained for one-half of the maximum imprisonment for the offence (not being an offence punishable with death) shall be released on bond, with or without sureties; the court may extend detention beyond half with reasons after hearing the Public Prosecutor, but never beyond the maximum period. BNSS 479 improves this: a first-time offender is released after one-third of the maximum, and the Superintendent of the jail must move the application — a provision the Supreme Court in 2024 directed to be applied retrospectively to all undertrials."));
c.push(H3("Non-bailable offences — Sec 437 (BNSS 480)"));
c.push(P("A person accused of a non-bailable offence **may** be released on bail by the officer in charge or by a court other than the High Court or Sessions Court, but **not** if there are reasonable grounds to believe he is guilty of an offence punishable with death or life imprisonment, or if the offence is cognizable and he was previously convicted of an offence punishable with death, life or seven years, or twice convicted of a cognizable offence punishable with three years or more — except that a person under sixteen, a woman, or a sick or infirm person may be released even then, and the court may release for any other special reason (437(1) provisos). Bail may be granted at any stage of the investigation, inquiry or trial if there are no reasonable grounds for believing the accused guilty but sufficient ground for further inquiry (437(2)). For offences punishable with seven years or more and for offences under IPC Chapters VI, XVI, XVII, the court imposes conditions — attendance, no similar offence, no tampering (437(3)). Reasons must be recorded (437(4)). Bail may be cancelled by the Magistrate who granted it (437(5)). If a summons-case trial is not concluded within sixty days from the first date of evidence, the accused in custody is released (437(6)). After the trial, if the court believes the accused is not guilty, it releases him on bond pending judgment (437(7)). The factors: nature and gravity of the accusation, severity of punishment, danger of absconding, character and standing, likelihood of repetition, tampering with witnesses, and the larger interest of the public and State (Prahlad Singh Bhati v. NCT of Delhi, 2001; State of U.P. v. Amarmani Tripathi, 2005)."));
c.push(H3("Special powers of the High Court and Sessions Court — Sec 439 (BNSS 483)"));
c.push(P("These courts may release any person in custody on bail, with or without conditions, and may set aside or modify conditions imposed by a Magistrate; in an offence exclusively triable by Sessions or punishable with life, notice must be given to the Public Prosecutor, and after 2018 the informant must be heard in rape cases under Sec 376(3), 376AB, 376DA, 376DB. They may also cancel bail and direct arrest (439(2)). Cancellation is not a routine review: it requires supervening circumstances — interference with witnesses, fleeing, similar offence — or an order that is perverse, granted without considering relevant material (Dolat Ram v. State of Haryana, 1995; Puran v. Rambilas, 2001; Mahipal v. Rajesh Kumar, 2020). Bail in economic offences is decided on the same principles though the gravity weighs more (Sanjay Chandra v. CBI, 2011: 'the object of bail is neither punitive nor preventative'). **Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI (2022)** grouped offences into four categories and directed that where the accused was not arrested during investigation and cooperated, bail after charge-sheet should ordinarily be granted; it also directed States to consider a separate Bail Act."));
c.push(H3("Anticipatory bail — Sec 438 (BNSS 482)"));
c.push(P("A person who apprehends arrest for a non-bailable offence may apply to the High Court or the Court of Session for a direction that in the event of arrest he shall be released on bail. The 2005 amendment listed factors — nature and gravity, antecedents, possibility of fleeing, whether the accusation is to injure or humiliate — allowed an interim order, required seven days' notice to the Public Prosecutor for the final hearing, and permitted the applicant's presence to be required at the final hearing. Conditions may include availability for interrogation, no inducement to witnesses, no leaving India without permission. It is barred for offences under Sec 376(3), 376AB, 376DA, 376DB (2018), under the SC/ST Act (Sec 18), and by many State amendments (Uttar Pradesh had deleted it from 1976 to 2019). **Sushila Aggarwal v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2020)** settled that anticipatory bail is not limited in time and can continue till the end of the trial, though the court may impose a limit in a particular case. It is not available after arrest (Gurbaksh Singh), nor to a proclaimed offender (Lavesh), and the applicant must show a reasonable apprehension, not a vague fear."));
c.push(H3("Bonds and sureties — Sec 440-450 (BNSS 484-496)"));
c.push(SEC("Sec 440-441A","Amount of bond fixed with due regard to the circumstances and never excessive; the High Court or Sessions may reduce it; bond of the accused and sureties; the surety declares the number of persons he stands surety for. Moti Ram v. State of M.P. (1978) — Krishna Iyer: excessive bail and the insistence on local sureties or cash amounts to denial of bail."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 442-444","Release on execution of the bond; the court may refuse an unfit surety; a surety may apply to be discharged, on which the accused is arrested and must furnish fresh sureties."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 445-446","A deposit of money may be accepted instead of a bond; on breach the court records the grounds, calls on the person to pay the penalty, and may recover it as a fine or imprison the defaulter up to six months; the surety's estate is liable after his death."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 446A-450","Cancellation of the bond on non-appearance and no fresh release without sureties; procedure where the surety is insolvent or dies; appeal from orders under Sec 446; the High Court or Sessions may direct levy of the amount due on a bond; deposit of the accused's own bond amount."));
c.push(CASE("Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia v. State of Punjab (1980) 2 SCC 565","Sec 438 discretion is wide and not to be fettered by judge-made conditions; anticipatory bail is not limited in time as a rule; the applicant need not make out a 'special case'."));
c.push(CASE("Hussainara Khatoon v. Home Secretary, State of Bihar (1979)","Speedy trial is part of Art 21; undertrials detained beyond the maximum sentence must be released; the genesis of Sec 436A and of legal aid."));
c.push(CASE("Moti Ram v. State of M.P. (1978)","Bail cannot be denied by fixing an unreasonable amount or demanding local sureties; monetary bail discriminates against the poor."));
c.push(CASE("Sushila Aggarwal v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2020) 5 SCC 1","Anticipatory bail need not be time-bound and ordinarily continues till the end of the trial; conditions depend on the case."));
c.push(CASE("Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI (2022) 10 SCC 51","Four categories of offences for bail; where the accused was not arrested during investigation and cooperated, arrest at the charge-sheet stage and denial of bail are unwarranted; reiterates Arnesh Kumar and calls for a Bail Act."));
c.push(DIG("Sec 436A was the first bail provision to be implemented through the National Judicial Data Grid and the e-Prisons database, which flag undertrials who have crossed half the maximum sentence; BNSS 479 requires jail superintendents to act on the flag. Electronic monitoring as a bail condition (ankle tags) has been recommended by the 268th Law Commission Report but is not yet in the Code; courts instead impose conditions like sharing live location on a phone, which the Supreme Court in Frank Vitus v. NCB (2024) held violates privacy. Bail orders are uploaded the same day and sent electronically to the jail (Supreme Court e-bail directions, 2021) to end the delay between order and release."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Anticipatory bail', 'Default bail', 'Sec 436A', 'Cancellation of bail' — section, principle, one case. Part B: 'Bail is the rule and jail is the exception — discuss with reference to Sec 437 and 439' or 'Explain the law relating to anticipatory bail' — write the section, the 2005 factors, the bars, Sibbia, Sushila Aggarwal, and the difference from regular bail. Part C: a woman accused of an offence punishable with life applies for bail to the Magistrate — answer that Sec 437(1) first proviso permits release; or an accused in a sixty-day summons trial still in custody — Sec 437(6)."));
c.push(LN());

module.exports=c;
